.TH BIOKIDS\-ADMIN 8 2026\-09\-30 "biokids 0.1.0" "BioKids"
.SH NAME
biokids\-admin \- BioKids recovery and administration commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B biokids\-admin
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B biokids\-admin
is for the moments the parent panel cannot help: a forgotten PIN, a daemon
that has stopped, or a child the panel knows about who cannot log in.
Run it as root.
.PP
It works without a network connection, and what it changes is written to
the audit log the parent panel shows.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B lockdown
Lock every child session that is logged in, through
.BR loginctl (1),
and record that in the audit log.
.B biokids\-lockdown.service
runs this when
.B biokids\-daemon
fails, so that a child is never left at an unsupervised machine.
It does not need the daemon.
If
.I /etc/biokids/config.toml
cannot be read, it locks using the built\-in defaults rather than not
locking at all.
.TP
.B status
Ask the daemon for each child's state: logged in or not, the minutes left
today, and the application in use.
Fails if the daemon is not answering.
.TP
.BR reset\-pin " [" \-\-stdin ]
Set a new parent PIN.
The PIN is asked for twice and never echoed.
It is not accepted as an argument, because arguments are visible to every
user on the machine.
With
.BR \-\-stdin ,
one line is read from standard input instead, for scripts.
This also clears any lockout left by wrong PIN attempts.
It is one of the two ways back in after a forgotten PIN; the other is the
recovery code the setup wizard showed.
.TP
.BI provision\-child " user"
Make sure the child account
.I user
exists and logs in to the BioKids launcher.
The panel does this when a parent adds a child; this command repairs it
when that could not happen.
An existing account is left alone, and nothing is ever deleted, except that
its name and picture on the login screen are set to the child's.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " display\-name"
The full name given to a newly created account.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-create
Only repair the session files; fail if the account does not exist.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace session files that have been edited since they were installed.
.RE
.TP
.B refresh\-greeter
Give every child's account their name and picture on the login screen,
through AccountsService.
The package runs this on every installation and upgrade; a child added in the
panel gets them when the account is made.
.TP
.B sync\-catalog
Load the application catalogue into the database.
It only adds: an application a parent disabled stays disabled, and one that
has left the catalogue is marked unavailable rather than removed.
The package runs this on every installation and upgrade.
.TP
.B version
Print the installed version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/biokids/config.toml .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
when the command could not do its job, and
.B 2
for a PIN that was rejected or a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/biokids/config.toml
Machine configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/biokids/biokids.db
Settings, time history and the audit log.
.TP
.I /run/biokids/daemon.sock
Where the daemon listens.
.TP
.I /usr/share/biokids/catalog/catalog.toml
The application catalogue.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR biokids\-daemon (8),
.BR biokids\-panel (8),
.BR loginctl (1)
